# Undo Stack — C

## Overview
A small command-line text editor built in C that uses a stack to implement Undo functionality.

## How it works
- Users can insert and delete characters.
- Each successful edit is recorded as an action.
- Actions are stored in a stack.
- Undo reverses the most recent action first (LIFO).

## Concepts practised
- Stack operations: push and pop
- Structs and enums in C
- Arrays and character manipulation
- Pointers and passing data between functions
- LIFO and state changes
- Testing and debugging

## Supported commands
- `insert <position> <character>`
- `delete <position>`
- `undo`
- `show`
- `quit`

## Limitations
- Fixed document capacity.
- Fixed undo-history capacity.
- Basic input handling; invalid positions and malformed commands are not fully validated.

## Running
Compile the C source file and run the resulting executable.

## Learning objective
Understand how a stack can store a history of actions and reverse state changes in a practical program.     